d the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x})-3(\\alpha {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-3)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc88a13f40fcec7eb3a828413e6a83bb2489c408e9b167eb0fcdda2cc136dba4"} {"question" "q-333171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1aef8361c78410ac53be90a1280497511be73fd3220b98943603a6a7db5f399a"} {"question" "q-333171" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc88a13f40fcec7eb3a828413e6a83bb2489c408e9b167eb0fcdda2cc136dba4"} {"question" "q-333171" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5e^{3x}-4e^x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc88a13f40fcec7eb3a828413e6a83bb2489c408e9b167eb0fcdda2cc136dba4"} {"question" "q-333178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=6{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc88a13f40fcec7eb3a828413e6a83bb2489c408e9b167eb0fcdda2cc136dba4"} {"question" "q-333178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{x}-4\\alpha {e}^{x} & =6{e}^{x} \\\\ -3\\alpha {e}^{x} & =6{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc88a13f40fcec7eb3a828413e6a83bb2489c408e9b167eb0fcdda2cc136dba4"} {"question" "q-333178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1aef8361c78410ac53be90a1280497511be73fd3220b98943603a6a7db5f399a"} {"question" "q-333178" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "dc88a13f40fcec7eb3a828413e6a83bb2489c408e9b167eb0fcdda2cc136dba4"} {"question" "q-333178" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{4x}-2e^x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256"